!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = treatment: tMCAO+UVB (UVB treatment was performed after tMCAO)
!Sample_molecule_ch1 = total RNA
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = A total of 33 male C57BL/6J mice from sham, tMCAO and tMCAO+UVB groups were used for library preparation to perform scRNA-seq. After 1 day ischemia-reperfusion, mice were anesthetized, fixed and perfused with cooling PBS to remove the blood. The ischemic hemisphere (tMCAO ipsilateral brain) was immediately dissected. Meanwhile, the skullcap and meninges were harvested, respectively. The skullcap and meninges samples were minced using a scalpel for digestion with 2 mg/mL collagenase (Shanghai Life iLab Biotech Co., Ltd. China) for 1 h and 35 min, respectively, and red blood cells were lysed with 2 mL blood red cell lysing reagent (Biosharp, Labgic Technology Co., Ltd.). The brain tissue was digested with 10U/mL papain (Worthington Biochemical Corporation, USA) for 30 minutes. And, red blood cells were lysed with 4 mL blood red cell lysing reagent (Biosharp, Labgic Technology Co., Ltd.). Debris Removal Solution（130-109-398, Miltenyi Biotec）was used to remove fragments. After the single-cell suspension was obtained, automated cell counters (Countstar Rigel S2, Shanghai Ruiyu Biotechnology Co., LTD, China) was employed for cell number count (cell viability >80%). Single-cell suspensions from all tissues (10 μl each) were counted and the cell concentration was adjusted to achieve a cell recovery of approximately 10,000 cells.Single-cell suspensions were loaded onto the Chromium Controller using the Chromium Single Cell 3′ Library & Gel Bead Kit (10x Genomics, PN-1000268).
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = The 3′ scRNA-seq libraries was established mainly following Chromium Single Cell 3' Reagent Kits User Guide (v3.1 Chemistry). Briefly, the gel bead emulsion was generated from the mixture of single cell suspension, gel beads and oils using the 10x Genomics chromium controller. Following droplet formation, the samples were transferred into PCR tubes. Reverse transcription was carried out using a T100 Thermal Cycler (Bio-Rad) according to the following protocol: incubation at 53℃ for 45 minutes, 85℃ for 5 minutes and finally held at 4℃. The resulting cDNA was then amplified and subjected to quality assessment using an Agilent Bioanalyzer 2100. The library was constructed by adding the P5 primer, Read 2 (primer site for sequencing read 2), Sample Index and P7 primer, respectively. Subsequently, the constructed library underwent quality control measures before being subjected to sequencing using the Illumina Novaseq 6000 platform (NovaSeq 6000 S4 Reagent Kit v1.5 with a 151-8-8-151 read setup). Details regarding sequencing depth and cell recovery are provided in Supplementary Table 2. Processing of scRNA-seq data was performed with the cellranger pipeline (v6.0.1, 10X Genomics) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. Briefly, raw bcl files were de-multiplex using the cellranger mkfastq pipeline. Subequent read alignments and transcript counting were performed individually for each sample using cellranger count pipeline with default parameters.
!Sample_data_processing = Downstream analysis was performed with R-package Seurat v4.3.0 using R v4.3.1. Low-quality cells and cell doublets were removed by filtering cells with few genes (<200), high number of genes (>5000) and high mitochondrial percentages (>20%) for each sample separately. After quality control the total remaining cell numbers used for further analysis were 27,018 brain cells, 27,353 meninges cells and on 22,937 skull bone marrow cells. (Supplementary Table 1). Filtered data were subjected to a recently described approach with reference-based mapping pipeline called Azimuth with default parameters. Azimuth provides well-curated cell reference map that input a count matrix and performs normalization, visualization, cell annotation and differential expression. For the mouse brain cell annotation, we used Azimuth provided mouse brain cell reference. For the mouse meninge and skull bone marrow cell annotation which Azimuth did not curate cell map reference, we built cell reference map by Azimuth-reference using Mazzitelli et al. mouse skull bone marrow scRNA-seq data for skull cell reference and Hove et al. dura scRNA-seq data for meninge cell reference. The brain, meninges and skull bone marrow cells were aligned between different treatment by using Harmony, a tool for batch correction that project cells into a shared embedding to cluster cell type across multiple conditions. The Harmony embeddings were used as input for t-distributed stochastic neighbor embedding (tSNE). The clusters were identified by the “FindNeighbors” and “FindClusters” functions in Seurat. To solve the automate Azimuth cell annotation inconsistence with clusters, we used “FindAllMarkers” from Seurat to identify marker genes to manually annotate clusters (Fig. 4h, i, j). To identify the differentially expressed genes (DEGs) across different conditions from merged dataset of brain, meninges and skull bone marrow, we used “FindMarker” function in Seurat to identify DEGs of tMCAO vs. Sham and tMCAO+UVB vs. tMCAO. The genes with |avg_log2FC| > 0.5 and p_val_adj < 0.05 were considered as significant differentially expressed genes.
!Sample_data_processing = Assembly: mm10
!Sample_data_processing = Supplementary files format and content: Tab-separated values files and matrix files
